WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.522 --> 00:00:04.272
[dramatic music]

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:11.233
- There is one kilo of Semtex
plastic explosive back there.

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:13.851
This ought to look good.

00:00:13.851 --> 00:00:16.768
[brief, muted explosion]

00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:20.130
Okay. That didn't look great on camera.

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:21.180
Neither did I.

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:24.900
The film industry has a lot of tricks
to make the explosions in movies

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:26.410
look like the audience expects them to,

00:00:26.410 --> 00:00:28.490
all fire and flame and devastation.

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:31.770
So now, we've seen what an
actual explosion looks like.

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:33.330
Let's see how Hollywood does it.

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:35.010
There are two parts to the trick,

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:37.930
and the first is what
you're actually blowing up.

00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:41.010
- We've just blown up
one kilo of Semtex 1A,

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:44.270
which is a PETN based plastic explosive.

00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:47.410
Now that's real military explosive.

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:48.680
So for a Hollywood effect,

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:52.050
we don't actually use the Semtex
because it does a lot of damage.

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:54.550
It's designed to do damage
for its military purpose.

00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:56.393
We don't want that.
We want something that looks nice

00:00:56.393 --> 00:00:58.300
but does the minimum
amount of damage possible.

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:00.950
So what we're going to use
is some detonating cord

00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:04.040
to burst a bag full of petrol,

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:05.680
and that will be ignited as it goes out

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:07.600
by an ignition charge off to one side.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:10.840
And we're going to do this in
three pots called mortar pots.

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:13.910
And that will direct the
fuel as it flies out.

00:01:13.910 --> 00:01:15.030
And then it gets ignited,

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:17.570
and that produces a
nice, big explosive effect.

00:01:17.570 --> 00:01:20.150
There are loads of flames
and fire and dirty smoke.

00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:22.050
We do that to just pointing
towards the camera.

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:23.970
We don't want to bother
sending it backwards

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:25.470
or to the sides, particularly.

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:28.230
We only want it where it's going
to do the effect that we want.

00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:30.620
For the three pots full of fuel,
we're probably going to use

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:32.240
less than 10 grams in each of them.

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:34.433
The reason we're getting a bigger effect
using less explosive

00:01:34.433 --> 00:01:35.581
is because of the fuel.

00:01:35.581 --> 00:01:38.165
We're putting that in there
because that will catch fire.

00:01:38.165 --> 00:01:40.526
Big, big yellow flames,
lots of dirty black smoke,

00:01:40.526 --> 00:01:43.098
because it's a mixture of
kerosene, petrol, diesel,

00:01:43.098 --> 00:01:45.371
and any other muck I happen
to have lying around(!)

00:01:45.371 --> 00:01:47.794
Those were in plastic bags in
front of the explosive charge.

00:01:47.794 --> 00:01:50.225
The explosive charge is just
there to propel the fuel out,

00:01:50.225 --> 00:01:53.070
vaporise it, make a big cloud of it,
which can then be ignited.

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:56.850
So the visual effect is from
the cloud of fuel igniting.

00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:59.490
And the explosive is just
used to create that cloud.

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:00.830
This is detonating cord.

00:02:00.830 --> 00:02:05.300
It's a thin plastic tube with
some fibre winding inside it,

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:08.097
but, down the very
centre, it's powdered PETN

00:02:08.097 --> 00:02:11.370
pentaethyle tetranitramine,
a high explosive.

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:14.680
And when this stuff goes off,
it will detonate along its length.

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:17.690
That's about six kilometres a second.
So it's pretty powerful.

00:02:17.690 --> 00:02:20.910
But, what we're going to do for
the Hollywood effect, is to reel it up,

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:23.640
and make like little flat pads of it.

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:25.790
When that detonates, the blast from it

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:29.370
will fracture the plastic bag
and spread the fuel out

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:31.770
and create our cloud of vapour
that we want to ignite.

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:33.830
The important thing to remember
is when you're using things

00:02:33.830 --> 00:02:35.810
like detonators, or the high
explosives in a mortar pot,

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:38.350
you need to separate it
from the actual steel pot.

00:02:38.350 --> 00:02:40.160
Because the shock waves from
the high explosive going off

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:41.950
can actually travel into the steel

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:44.260
and actually peel bits of it off
and create fragmentation.

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:47.543
So you need to put a lump of foam
between the explosive and the mortar pot

00:02:47.543 --> 00:02:52.380
to make sure that it doesn't break up and
damage the actors you might have close by.

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:56.450
- With all the explosives in place,
the second trick is how you film it.

00:02:56.450 --> 00:02:58.590
By moving the camera back and zooming in,

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:01.650
you make it look like your actor is
much, much closer to the explosion

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:03.420
and it means the whole frame is filled up

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:05.220
while everyone's still at a safe distance.

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:06.700
Then, you shoot in slow motion.

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:08.240
You make the explosion seem even longer

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:10.800
by shooting from multiple angles
and repeating it several times,

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:13.840
and use all the cinematic lens
and colour tricks you can

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:16.510
to make it look...
well, more cinematic.

00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:19.539
And you tell your actor not to flinch.

00:03:19.539 --> 00:03:22.456
[long, repeated, slow explosive blasts]

00:03:35.390 --> 00:03:38.310
Also, you hope he doesn't
give a cheesy grin to camera.

00:03:38.310 --> 00:03:41.630
Like, we had one take, and
what I did was...

00:03:41.630 --> 00:03:43.850
I should've done sort-of
cold steel glare down the camera.

00:03:43.850 --> 00:03:46.960
I just had a grin on my face
cause I'm firing a thing!

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:47.823
Did that work?

00:03:48.730 --> 00:03:50.600
I was looking this way.

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:52.350
I really wanted to look at the explosion.

00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:55.163
Oh, there is... there is a lot
on fire back there, isn't there.

00:03:55.163 --> 00:03:55.996
Wow.

